A||||30||Ken Wittlief||WOW my fiero is squirrely in the snow!||||10||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||12-28-2001||11:27 PM||||Finally got to drive my fully stock '85SE V6/auto in a few inches of snow today.<P> Not real pleased with how it handles :c0 - the rear end breaks loose fairly easy, but the worst part, the front wheels break loose really easy when you try to turn, and once they loose traction the car goes completely straight, no matter where the front wheels are pointed.<P> I finally figured out that if you turn the front wheel a little too hard, and the goose the throttle a bit, then the back end will come around some and you can do a little bit of a power drift.<P> Ive driven several air cooled VWs over the years, and I love the way they handle in the snow - But I think its gonna take a while to get use to the Fiero in the white stuff.||||reg||10
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||12-28-2001||11:36 PM||||What tires do you have? I find that the tires make a huge difference in snow handling.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88coupeV6.jpg">       <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>    <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/nefa.htm" TARGET=_blank>Join New England Fiero Association</A>||||reg||5
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||12-28-2001||11:46 PM||||I had to go look - they are Michelin MX-L - same size on all four wheels.<P>they look like 4 season treads, not real agressive.<P> I got the feeling when driving that the back wheels were trying to push the car straight forward, almost like it has positraction.||||reg||10
Z||000003||grgoyl86||12-29-2001||08:04 AM||||I have found, from personal experience, that skinnier tires work much better in the snow. They look bad, and don't handle as well on dry pavement, but when there's snow on the ground, WOW! what a difference.<BR>Of course, a good all-season tread, or even a winter tread helps.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Galen||12-29-2001||09:46 AM||||and dont try to turn at 55-65mph on a turn you'd normally take(and has signs posted) at 25-35mph  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/galen-sig-2k2.gif">    <BR><B><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT></B> - <A HREF="http://www.fierolinks.f2s.com/index.shtml" TARGET=_blank>Fierolinks</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||TRiAD||12-29-2001||10:45 AM||||195 series SNOW tires seem to be the best answer for this. the 215s are just too much to be able to apply any pressure to the contact patch. <P>Good luck!<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://centralillinoisimports.org/uploads/files/SigRear4.gif">  <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B><BR>AIM ~ Michael Blue 72</I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||12-29-2001||11:37 AM||||You might want to try to put a little bit of weight in the front of the car.  Try throwing a bag of sand or something in with the spare tire.  The Fiero is rather light up front which can cause the front wheels to ride on top of the snow instead of digging into it.  Otherwise you just have to take your time in the white stuff.  Also watch for deep snow... not much ground clearance!  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||12-29-2001||02:05 PM||||That is why my Fieros go into storage during snow time.  I could only use 3rd,4th & 5th gears in the winter snow.  The GT felt like I was driving on skis.. no fun.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 12-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||12-29-2001||03:11 PM||||Heh, like Galen said, you can't take '35mph' curves at 80 anymore  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR>PS: random scary fact.  with my new engine, third gear on the getrag is going to be good for... uh... ~140mph...  Yeikes.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||fldevel||12-29-2001||10:34 PM||||what the hell is snow?? ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Master_Sushi||12-29-2001||11:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>with my new engine, third gear on the getrag is going to be good for... uh... ~140mph...  Yeikes.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Wow thats insane. What would 4th be then? Like 180?<P>Whats the top RPM you can hit with it?<P>Sorry I'm a bit OT.....||||reg||1
Z||000011||BN Boomer||12-29-2001||11:44 PM||||Fieros just plain suck in snow and ice. The rear end always wants to break lose and beat you to your destination. With the low ground clearance of Fiero, you are just begging to get hung up in ruts, small drifts, or other accumulations. 4WD, AWD, and FWD are much better choices for driving in snow and ice.||||reg||13
Z||000012||842m8||12-30-2001||12:58 AM||||I drove my car for the first time today in the snow! AHHHH what a nightmare, the slightest touch of the gas pendle and my back end is sliding sideways, thats soo not good.... even while shifting the back end would whip out. Some guy in a subaru wrx laughed at me as I slid by going sideways and he drove perfectly fine LOL||||reg||1
Z||000013||JohnF||12-30-2001||08:24 AM||||Every vehicle sucks in the snow.  <P>Yesterday there was a 100+ vehicle, at least 6 death pile-up in the snow country that "knows-how-to-drive-in-the-snow". We hear! <P>As to 4WD and such, I found out in Ottawa they still suck, just not as much as 2WD!<P>Just another reason why we outlawed snow here long ago.    <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://photo.msn.s8.com/MS8zLzAvMS8xMDg3LzE1NS8zMi9INU54UkJyRnlPbnZkaDdvRENhLW13/2a787ccc86b5da461605f26eef23ff8a/clbk=0h9xCkj4Yyo6VbTdcNrmhglJSkAWB6C91volMeDlp0h*oii8TPGa20kyOxpdcqzP8qgFSFmsURSXXLzrLUqyll5ofimdP92*FNFuzBBDM1PEajQxQ*Q8WWq*rfvz6!x4pUtE2EsH24ClDyP3oWXL*C!sOjXA63RMH7rLXNlHSu0Yga0L9A7awE0Vo4vjfHksI0c5clA2zMM!vGsSGqWEfH6rUngsCm7dmRIGCL4B98JxdRCo19jb2zHG1Q2F8!3f0GHxu0h66objRD*CveMNtxaf03Gv9Wi1/jpg.jpg">   <P>You can have all the SPEED you can afford!||||reg||1
Z||000014||BN Boomer||12-30-2001||09:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B>Every vehicle sucks in the snow.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, these vehicles don't suck, they blow!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.hazegray.org/rail/snow/rotary/bn03.jpg"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ken Wittlief||12-30-2001||10:23 AM||||I have the impression that the tires are too wide for effective traction in the snow - all my aircooled VWs had stock width tires, and they are only about 5" wide - you want the tires to dig down to the pavement, not to float on top of the snow.<P> Im sure a set of studded tires would turn the fiero into a snowmobile extrodenair, but then Im looking at BIG $$$ for 4 snow tires and a set of wheels to keep them on.<BR> <BR>the mid engine rear wheel drive 40/60 weight distribution should make the fiero an excellent machine in the snow.<P> I also figured out how to burn out your headlight motors - try turning your headlights on with the doors frozen shut - no I havent dont it yet - will have to remember to keep the headlight doors clear, since or its bound to happen sooner or later.||||reg||10
Z||000016||Mach10||12-30-2001||12:51 PM||||Took me a little while, but a set of Snow-Tires, and a bit of technique modification later...<P>I would NOT drive anything else in the snow again. The Fiero ROCKS for slippy conditions, but you have to watch for a few things:<P>1) BOTH motors, the 4cyl, and 6cyl, are a little torquey. As such, you need to be gentle with the throttle. <BR>2) The front-end is a little light. Toss a 30kg bag of sand in the front like I did. Makes a WORLD of difference (and lowers the front about 1.5"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )<BR>3) 195/75/14 SNOW tires on all 4 corners is your FRIEND. I can stick my car better to the road than anything I've seen yet. I have the scraped paint on my rear bumper to prove it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>4) When cornering, you still power through the corner. BUT you need to make some changes. Power out a little LATER than normal. Instead of at the apex of the turn, wait a little longer. It'll keep the car in line.<BR>5) If you lose the back end, do NOT make sudden corrections. You WILL over-correct. Feather the throttle and brakes, and nudge the wheel in the proper direction. Trust me... It works. Spent 1.5hrs in an icy parking lot perfecting this. Shopping carts make GREAT lane markers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"><BR>6) If you DO lose it, lock the tires when the car is almost straight. This will slow/stop your spinout. Correct as necessary<BR>7) If you need to stop, but can't because of ice, the curb can be your best friend. otherwise, straddle the middle of the lane. Usually less packed ice.<P>Have fun, but don't scrub another car... They are starting to get rare  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000017||Ferrari_cdn||12-30-2001||04:50 PM||||What Mach said is right.  I know that Fieros are difficult to handle in snow, unless you do some modifications, like sandbags and snow tires, then its good.  My friend got stuck in a street parking spot with his Lumina, took him 2 minutes to get out. (had to go, reverse, go, reverse, to help get out).  I went to the same parking spot to pick up my friend, and took 10 seconds to get out.  Maybe a little tire spin but I got out a lot faster and easier.  And that was with my regular 215 tires.  (all season)<P>Just be careful, sandbags in the front DID help.  I know it did, I got about 75 lbs of it under the front hood.<P>I'm sure Mach had fun in the parking lot.  He doesn't like being light on the throttle.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>BN BOOMER:  With the low ground clearance of Fiero, you are just begging to get hung up in ruts, small drifts, or other accumulations.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was riding with Mach and we were going to an auto parts store.  It was the day after we had a HEAVY snowfall, and the city plowed the snow off the road.  They didn't clear the cleared snow that ended up on the turn onto the parking lot.   Mach rammed through the snow (the aero nose was pushing it).  He was deep in snow, but he was still creeping forward.  Got out of it.  Didn't get stuck.  There might be chances you will get stuck, but just be careful.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif">  AIM: CanadianFiero<p>[This message has been edited by Ferrari_cdn (edited 12-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Ken Wittlief||12-30-2001||05:36 PM||||Iagree 100% the most important thing you can do is spend time in a big empty parking lot getting the feel for the car by spinning it out and then recovering it - that is the place to learn to do it, not on a two lane road going 45mph with trucks coming the other way :c0<P> Im wondering now if narrower tires will also improve the mpgs on my fiero. Im averaging 20 to 25mph with it almost all the time - Back when I had a Rabbit I replaced the stock tires when they wore out with the lower profile, wider ones, and I noticed a significant reduction in mpgs. <P> Maybe I will keep my eyes out for a  set of steel wheels for my fiers and pick up a set of snow tires for it<P> how much does a 75 lb bag of kitty litter cost?! in addittion to adding weight to the front end, kitty litter is priceless if you get stuck in the snow or ice, you throw some infront of the wheels on the ground and its like magic traction dust - if you dont keep a bag of it in your trunk, at least keep a cool whip container full in your car.||||reg||10
Z||000019||BN Boomer||12-30-2001||11:24 PM||||Sorry, but anyone who thinks that putting snow tires and a bag of sand or kitty litter on a Fiero makes it good for snowy, icy, and slippery road conditions either doesn't have very much driving experience or is seriously deluded. The main problem is that the Fiero's already inherent tendency to oversteer is seriously exacerbated on snow and ice, Yeah, if it's all you've got it can probably get by in a pinch if driven with extrem caution, but there are much better choices. And using the curb to control your movement? Doesn't that sound like the perfect way to damage your control arms, ball joints, wheel bearings, tie rods, grounf effects, etc? Not a very good plan of action, in my humble opinion.<P>Trust me, in the snow, my 4WD Suburban would put any Fiero ever made to shame. It has far superior traction, groud clearance, stopping ability, and tracks better through snow and ice. In fact, the Suburban was the only vehicle that could even get out of our driveway a few years ago following a heavy snow. <P>If anyone wants to suggest otherwise, I suggest that next week they follow me over Lookout Pass on I-90 crossing between Idaho and Montana. Traversing it in a Fiero in the middle of winter would blast the Pucker-O-Meter right of the chart.<P>Fieros are best left in the garage during snowy winters. You would be far better served by even an inexpensive older FWD vehicle.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Mach10||12-31-2001||01:43 AM||||     <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BN Boomer:<BR><B>Sorry, but anyone who thinks that putting snow tires and a bag of sand or kitty litter on a Fiero makes it good for snowy, icy, and slippery road conditions either doesn't have very much driving experience or is seriously deluded. The main problem is that the Fiero's already inherent tendency to oversteer is seriously exacerbated on snow and ice, Yeah, if it's all you've got it can probably get by in a pinch if driven with extrem caution, but there are much better choices. And using the curb to control your movement? Doesn't that sound like the perfect way to damage your control arms, ball joints, wheel bearings, tie rods, grounf effects, etc? Not a very good plan of action, in my humble opinion.<P>Trust me, in the snow, my 4WD Suburban would put any Fiero ever made to shame. It has far superior traction, groud clearance, stopping ability, and tracks better through snow and ice. In fact, the Suburban was the only vehicle that could even get out of our driveway a few years ago following a heavy snow. <P>If anyone wants to suggest otherwise, I suggest that next week they follow me over Lookout Pass on I-90 crossing between Idaho and Montana. Traversing it in a Fiero in the middle of winter would blast the Pucker-O-Meter right of the chart.<P>Fieros are best left in the garage during snowy winters. You would be far better served by even an inexpensive older FWD vehicle.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>In order:<BR>I question your own driving experience if you believe that tires don't make a difference. I also question as to whether you understand WHY I put more weight up front. <BR>There is no oversteer "problem" with the Fiero. If you've ever taken the car SERIOUSLY through it's paces, you would see that the ONLY time it will oversteer is if you lay on the gas too late in a corner, or if you drop a gear and coast through a corner. The Fiero's main tendency is to UNDERSTEER due to the lighter front end. Slippery conditions don't CHANGE a car's handling characteristics. Rather, they AMPLIFY them. The Fiero is a VERY predictable package on the ice. FWD is woefully unpredictable. Admittedly, when the car is sideways, it IS easier to pull it out, but the Fiero won't go sideways unless you are doing something wrong. <P>Now, the shot at the "curb" Read it again. This is in an EMERGENCY. I'd rather get an alignment than have to use a tire-iron to separate 2 cars. Do you HONESTLY believe that I drive around with a wheel on the curb? Don't be a retard. Want pictures of my rims? NO CURB RASH. <P>You are talking about a TRUCK. We are talking about CARS. I fail to see the comparison. FURTHERMORE, you are comparing a 2wd <B>CAR</B> with a 4wd <B>TRUCK</B>. Funny you should mention trucks. I've seen the towing company pull out 3 trucks out of the ditches on my street. A Ford Explorer, A Dodge 4x4, and a late 80's Chevy 4x4. No cars yet. What does that tell you? I hate to be the one to break it to you, but while your TRUCK excels at ploughing through 4ft drifts, it couldn't corner around the MOON if you had a team of friends pushing it. This is why the ditches are lined with trucks every year. <P>I'll be the first to admit that I don't have the most driving experience out of the two of us. In fact, my Grandmother probably has more driving experiece than both of us together... But she's STILL a shitty driver. I learned to drive in Winnipeg. I DRIVE in Winnipeg. We average 110-140 cm of snow per year. I think that firmly plants us 'Peggers in the "snow-bound" cities. We typically have snow from October through to mid april. We spend more time shovelling snow than wearing t-shirts (although us die-hards don't don sweaters till -30ºC).<P>I do agree though... My Fiero SHOULD be in the garage. I've already had one crazy *******  kiss my rear bumper, and I shudder to even THINK about salt damage. But I don't want to stow it because of safety. I am perfectly comfortable, and perfectly secure in my car. Say what you like. I'll still take my Fiero with snow tires and a sangbag over any other of the cars in our possession. Yes, I can find better. But will YOU lend me 25,000US for a Subaru Impreza? Didn't think so.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 12-31-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000021||Ken Wittlief||12-31-2001||09:39 AM||||I thought all they get in washington is rain rain rain :c)<P> I grew up and learned to drive in buffalo, NY - in aircooled VWs<P> driving in the snow and ice is just like driving on dry asphalt when you go around a turn so fast that your tires break loose - then you are in a power slide.<P> with a rear wheel drive vehicle you can power the car through the curve - if the car is mid-engine or rear engine you are in good shape because you can steer through the curve and apply power to push the car towards the center of the turn<P> if a rear wheel drive car starts to spin on a straightaway, you can regain control by letting off on the gas a little - the rear wheels then pull the car straight again.<P> a front wheel drive car can not power slide through a turn, once the front wheels break loose they do not have enought traction to both hold you in the turn and apply additional force to push you towards the center of the turn<P> and on a straightaway, if a front wheel drive car starts to spin out, you have to SPEED UP a little to pull the front end forwards and straighten the car out again -lets see, you are already going too fast and now you have to speed up more! This sounds 'better' to anyone.<P> Four wheel drive is the worst option when it comes to handling - I cant tell you how many 4WD owners simply do NOT understand that you ONLY use 4WD when you are stuck in the snow - you should NEVER drive around in 4WD mode<P> BECAUSE when a 4WD vehicle startst to spin out you have LOST IT. If you speed up a little all four wheels speed up, if you back off the gas all four wheels back off - there is no force difference between the front and back wheels and there is NO WAY to recover from a power slide or spin in a 4WD vehicle.<P> THAT is why you see them in the ditches all the time in heavy snow - the owners put them in 4WD and take off like crazy "Wow we got lots of traction" and they get going faster than they can navigate, and sooner or later they spin out or enter a turn too fast and the fat lady sings :c0<P>  WIth my VW bugs, and I fully expect with my fiero, once you get the feeling of controlling the car while its sliding - using the gas pedal to push the car where you want it to go, its a total blast to drive - it feels more like you are zipping around in a powerboat with an outboard motor than driving a car.<P> The Aircooled VWs I had when I learned to drive were mostly stock - in the summer they were dogs to drive around - 55HP on a 2000 lb car is not exactly a german sports car<P> but in the winter I was in my glory. I have never gotten stuck in a VW, and my landlord yelled at me when I was in college cause I never shoveled my side of the driveway all winter (in buffalo) I didnt need to, the bug went right through the stuff.<P> Imagine what it would be like if you could drive your fiero or porche 911 around all summer sliding through turns and curves all the time - well, if you learn how to handle a fiero (or a bug) you can do that all winter, whenever there is snow on the pavement.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 12-31-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000022||84Bill||12-31-2001||10:16 AM||||Heheheh Fiero + Snow = Fun<BR>but sounds like you may need an alignment. I have passed idiots in 4wd SUVs that get stuck on small grades. The 88's are the BEST in the snow, hands down.||||reg||14
Z||000023||Ken Wittlief||12-31-2001||10:25 AM||||you might be right about the alignment - you can usually feel if your tow-in is out in the snow, by turning the front wheel slightly while driving you can feel when one wheel is 'catching' and the other is slipping, and then when the other wheel catches.<P> the pavement is all dry here now - but I will have to check for this - I hate to even think about getting my front end aligned, or the rear - I dont think any of the suspension components have ever been replaced on my '85SE. (except the shocks)||||reg||10
Z||000024||BN Boomer||12-31-2001||10:43 AM||||Mach10:<P>Did I say that tires don't make any difference or are you just trying to put words in my mouth? Yeah, that what I thought. I also understand why you would put additional weight on your frontend. To achieve a better weight ratio and help the front tires steer and brake more effectively.<P>The Fiero is quite sensitive to any throttle change in mid-turn. The rear end of the 84-87s is also prone to bump-steer. Either of these make for a troublesome vehicle to operate in the snow and ice. Couple them together and they make the vehicle an exceptionally poor choice to drive in snow and ice. It's no secret that once a Fiero loses it, you pretty much just have to hang on and pray while going along for the rest of the ride.<P>I guess I missed the part where you said to use the curb "for emergencies" only. If you go back and reread your post, it does sound as if you casually suggest it, giving the appearance that's it's no big deal and can be done on a routine basis.<P>We're only talking about cars here? Is that in some secret message at the beginning of the thread? I thought we were simple talking about driving in the snow. Silly me. But the fact is, a 4WD truck or SUV is a much better vehicle than a Fiero to drive in the snow and ice. All that your stories tell me is that there is no law preventing idiots from driving SUVs. Wow, a vehicle driven beyond it's limitations wound up in a ditch. Your examples would be akin to me relating a few stories about Fieros catching on fire and then suggesting that you draw the conclusion that all Fieros are fire-prone deathtraps. Nice try, but no cigar. <P>You obviously have little experience driving pickups or SUVs, don't you? Fact is, it's d*mn near impossible to get my Suburban to spin out. Trust me, I try in the snow and at the beach all of the time. With it's long wheelbase and near 50/50 weight distribution, it's so stable and controllable that it's just no fun. Put it in 4WD and it just pulls itself in circles. The BFG A/Ts (oops, there's that tire thing again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) just want to bite in and take hold. A Fiero would be smashed up against the curb long before it would get anywhere near to taking a corner as well in the snow or ice. I've also found 4WDs to be easier to slow down and stop because you have more mechanical resistance that helps slow you down once you let of the gas (and you don't have to worry about torque steer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ). <P>Now if you want to talk about the short wheelbase CJ-5 I had a while ago, well, that thing could get pretty squirrely in the snow. But nowhere near as loose as my Fiero. And for some reason I just can't picture a Fiero breaking trail through a couple of feet of snow like that old Jeep could.<P>Ken:<P>You are aware that there is more to Washington than the coast side, right? Like a whole eastern half that gets tons of snow and big mountains (and not those itty bitty foothills that north-eastern types call mountains  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ). Geez, we even have glaciers here in Washington. Like I said, follow me over Lookout Pass in a couple of days. You'll be begging for the streets of Buffalo.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>FWD cars get better traction than typical front engine/RWD cars. You also have the benefit of the front end pulling you around corners rather than the rearend trying to push itself out. The whole idea is to avoid spinning out in the first place. Having driven numerous examples of both in all manner of weather, I can say that in my own opinion FWD beats RWD in the snow and ice hands down. AWD and 4WD trounces both of them. <P>Guys, don't take any of this as an assault on the Fiero. It's a great little car. Just not in the snow and ice. Let's be honest. There are much better choices than the Fiero for driving in snow and ice. Just like I wouldn't suggest trying to blast through a tight cloverleaf on a nice day with a Suburban.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000025||Ken Wittlief||12-31-2001||12:03 PM||||gee I dont know - Ill bet if you take the tires off your SUV and put them on a Fiero then you might be surprized<P> and Ill bet a narrow set of studded tires on a fiero would be a force to be reconed with<P> I dont have any need to drive over mountain passes here in upstate NY, but there are some hills here and there that most people 'go around' when the salt truck hasnt done its thing yet.<P> I think you are missing the point - a car like a fiero or an older VW bug can be an absolute BLAST to drive in the snow, once you get it setup right.<P> A friend of mine use to live in NE PA back when I had my VWs - the first big snowfall of winter I would go down there and we would play cat and mouse onthe back roads of pennsylvania, and on the logging roads through the state game lands<P> one time we were going down this road that hadnt been plowed with about a foot of light snow on it, and it was coming up the front hood of my bug and over the roof, like the car was burrowing through the snow - I had to turn the wipers on could barely see where I was going - but I didnt dare stop either.<P> another thing we use to do was take our bugs to the mall on sunday mornings after a big snow - they plowed the mall up and down, leaving 4 to 5 foot high snow banks every 50 feet from one end to the other - we use to race the distance, going through about 10 snowbanks - you had just enough distance to get up enough speed to blast through them, come through the other side, and get your speed up for the next one.<P> If I want the absolute best machine for driving in the snow I will get a snow mobile, or one of those things the ski resorts have with tank treads.<P> With the right car and enough practice in an empty parking lot you can learn to drive a car in the snow with it skidding and sliding all the time - its a whole different way of driving - and it really is fun<P> and you can learn to control the car that way so you dont crash into stuff<P> my first post in this thread wasnt to declare that fieros are no good in the snow - I was pointing out that with a 135HP engine and auto, wide tires and the cars short wheel base the car is squirrely<P> down hill skis are also squirrely - so are jet skis - so is a motorcross bike when its 20 feet in the air :c)<P> Im sure that once I make a few tweaks to my fiero, and get use to it in the snow, I will have a blast with it- Turns the normal 15 mile commute to work in the morning into a genuine adventure!||||reg||10
Z||000026||johnt671||12-31-2001||01:37 PM||||60 series and above tires are not good for snow.  (except maybe the new ultra snow tires)  A taller narrow tire has more weight on the tread patch then a wide tire and cuts through the snow better.  A four wheel driver told me in a bad storm in NY that he didn't like the 4 wheel drive because it gave him confidence to go faster, but he wasn't able to stop or corner any better than 2 wheel drive.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Ferrari_cdn||12-31-2001||03:43 PM||||Its a more fair comparsion if you compare a car to a car.  And I can say this, I used to drive a FWD '82 Olds Omega.  My RWD Fiero could handle snow better than my olds did.  I remember getting stuck in snow drifts in my olds, and my girlfriend getting pissed off.<P>Yes, the Fiero IS sensitive to throttle, but, if you know what you're doing and drive SMART, you're not going to spin out.  I've tried to test myself by pushing limits here and there on city streets (usually at 3 a.m., no one around on the streets).  I still have NOT spun out.  Always slided sideways and got it back in control.  I know I WILL be too late eventually, but, I'm saying if you know how to handle a Fiero, then it's okay.  I've had a few occasions where I ended up oversteering and my back end slipped out 45 degrees over, but I turned my wheel and she was sliding like that for a bit then straightened out.  Those driving was on purpose, and at parking lots.<P>I said before, and will say it again, drive SMART.  Then you won't lose control and hit anyone, you'll just have to worry about people losing control and hitting you.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif">  AIM: CanadianFiero<p>[This message has been edited by Ferrari_cdn (edited 12-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||Mattyman||12-31-2001||04:13 PM||||Man, I drove my 87 coupe on the snow covered roads for the first time and she was great.  I have 195/75/14's on it, they have a little meat and I really couldn't complain.  I just took it a little easy and no problems at all.  All I drove before were awd vehicles like an Asrto van and a RAV4, this was my first time driving a rearwheel drive car in the snow and it went well.  It even makes it up my driveway which is a killer.  Its about 1/3 of a mile long and very steep.  I make it up with minimal wheelspin.  You just gotta be light on the gas pedal when starting  and light on the break when stopping.  The car also stopped good too, I slammed on the breaks a few times to get a feel for it and it barely slid at all and if it did it went straight.  So im sataisfied with it.<P>------------------<BR>You gotta stand for Something or your gonna fall for everything.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Ken Wittlief||12-31-2001||09:56 PM||||the only car Ive ever had spin out on me in a 'bad way' was my '69 Cougar 350 Windsor w/auto<P> I bought it in florida when I was in the coast guard in WP beach, and I moved back to Buffalo on thankgiving day - I went out that evening to get something from the convience store around 11PM, and it was starting to snow.<P> In florida I loved the fact that you could be half a block from the corner, have a light turn yellow on you, punch the cougar to the floor and still make it through before the light turned red - well sure enough, Im on a 4 lane street and the light turns yellow, I punch it out of habit and next thing I know Im doing multiple 360s down the middle of the street.<P> good thing there was no traffic!<P> I also spun the thing out on the NYS thruway once going 65mph - raining and freezing, and I couldnt quite recover it all the way, ended up on the shoulder sideways.<P> engine in the front, rear wheel drive - not good up here :c0<P> I sold that car after a year BTW and went back to air cooled VWs - the early cougars had a front end problem - the towers spread out and you couldnt keep the front wheels aligned unless you built a cage over the top of the engine.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 12-31-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000030||BN Boomer||12-31-2001||10:23 PM||||He he, yeah, Ken, I'll bet if I could fir 265/75R16s on a Fiero I <I>would</I> be surprised! I wonder what that would do for handling? Even if I could, I would still be left with better traction tires on a car that only has 1RWD.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Start out with a 4WD or AWD platform, add similarly better tires, and you'll still be way ahead of any 2WD platform.<P>Just because you don't have to drive across mountain passes or through hilly areas doesn't negate the fact that some of us of do or that the Fiero is not an ideal choice for doing so.<P>Perhaps I did miss the point that driving a Fiero in the snow could be a blast. Maybe I'm just looking at it from a different perspective. I need to go to work, go to the store, get to the hospital in an emergency, etc. When I go hunting, having to work through a foot or two of fresh snow is not uncommon. I want rock solid reliable, capable transportation first. That means not having to depend on "powersliding" around corners to succesfully negotiate them. Like I said, the goal of driving in snow and ice should be to never lose traction in the first place, not constantly depending on skill at regaining control once it has been lost. Years of driving everything from little Fieros to 10 ton 8x8 Army trucks has taught me that 4WD and AWD vehicles excel in slippery/low traction conditions.<P>I'm not doubting that within their limitations, driving a VW Bug or a Fiero in the snow could be done successfully and may even be fun. But there are much better choices available that don't suffer from such narrow limitations. If a Fiero is all you have to get around in and you can live within it's limitations, then by all means do so. Driving around hills isn't an option for some of us.<P>Ferrari-cdn, we're talking about driving in snow and ice. Who cares about whether or not it's a fair comparison? All that matters to me is how ell I can get around. In the end, experience has dictacted to me that my 4WD will still be moving forward well after the Fiero is hopelessly stuck.<P>Mattyman, "I just took it a little easy" is probably the best remark made about driving in snow and ice so far. And it is applicable regardless of what you are driving.<P>||||reg||1
